<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/foundational/the-cold-war">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Between 1945 and 1991 the world was organised around one confrontation: the United States
    and the Soviet Union, allies against Hitler who became enemies before his body was cold,
    facing each other with <strong>the two largest armies, two incompatible ideologies, and —
    from 1949 — nuclear arsenals capable of ending civilisation</strong>. They never fought each
    other directly; that is why the conflict is "cold". But it was a real war in every other
    sense: fought through proxy battles in Korea, Vietnam, Afghanistan and Africa; through a
    space race, sports and propaganda; through blockades, coups and walls — and it killed
    millions of people who were not Soviet or American.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Cold War matters for three reasons. It explains the present: NATO, the divided Koreas,
    Taiwan's status and today's nuclear arsenals are all its deposits. It invented a new logic
    of conflict — <strong>deterrence</strong>, winning by <em>not</em> fighting — which still
    shapes international relations. And it is the best available case study in the question the
    syllabus asks about every international crisis: <em>who was responsible?</em> — including
    the possibility that the answer is "both" or "neither, the system did it".
  </p>
  <p>
    The widget gives the four frames of the story: the standoff, the nuclear logic, the
    competition that built things, and the anticlimactic end.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the standoff, the logic, and the ending" widget="equationStep" config={coldCfg} />

  <h2>Why the alliance broke down in 1945</h2>
  <p>
    The US–Soviet alliance was always a marriage of necessity against a common enemy, and the
    enemy's defeat removed the reason for it. What remained were incompatible visions of the
    post-war world. The Soviet view, shaped by invasion through Poland twice in a generation
    (1914, 1941) and by some 27 million dead, was that <strong>security required friendly
    governments on its western border</strong> — and, in Stalin's ideology, that capitalism and
    communism were destined to compete. The American view, shaped by the Depression's lesson and
    by Woodrow Wilson's unfinished project, was that <strong>prosperity and peace required open
    trade, self-determination and collective security</strong> through the new United Nations —
    principles that were genuinely held and that also happened to suit the world's richest
    economy.
  </p>
  <p>
    The specific breakdown of 1945–47 had concrete steps. At <strong>Yalta</strong> (February
    1945) and <strong>Potsdam</strong> (July 1945) the Allies had deferred hard questions about
    Germany and Eastern Europe with hopeful ambiguities — "free and unfettered elections" for
    Poland that Stalin never intended to hold. Truman, who took office in April 1945 knowing
    little of Roosevelt's private understandings, grew steadily harder after confrontations over
    Poland and Iran; the US atomic monopoly (demonstrated at Hiroshima in August 1945) gave
    Stalin an additional reason to grab territory fast, and to spy on the Manhattan Project. In
    February 1946, Stalin gave a speech declaring capitalism and communism irreconcilable, and
    US diplomat George Kennan sent his "Long Telegram" advising that the USSR was expansionist
    but patient, and should be <strong>contained</strong>. In March 1946 Churchill, in Missouri,
    named the thing: <em>"From Stettin in the Baltic to Trieste in the Adriatic, an iron curtain
    has descended across the continent."</em> By 1947 the language of alliance had been replaced
    by the language of blocs.
  </p>

  <h2>How the USSR took Eastern Europe by 1948</h2>
  <p>
    The Soviet Union did not usually conquer Eastern Europe a second time — its army was already
    there, having driven the Wehrmacht back to Berlin at colossal cost. What followed was a
    takeover in stages, differently paced by country. In Poland, Romania and Bulgaria, coalition
    governments were formed, then communists took the interior and defence ministries, then
    opponents were intimidated, rigged elections followed, and by 1947–48 the regimes were
    fully Stalinist. In Czechoslovakia — the most genuinely democratic case — communists won a
    plurality in free elections in 1946, then seized total power by force in the
    <strong>Prague coup of February 1948</strong>; the democratic foreign minister Jan Masaryk
    died in a fall days later. Yugoslavia under Tito broke with Stalin in 1948 while staying
    communist — proof that "Eastern Europe" was never monolithic. Only Finland, which accepted
    limits on its foreign policy but kept its democracy, escaped the pattern.
  </p>
  <p>
    The machinery had a name after 1947: <strong>Cominform</strong> (coordinating communist
    parties) and later <strong>Comecon</strong> (the economic bloc, 1949), tying the satellites
    to Moscow. Winston Churchill's "iron curtain" described the result, but the tactical
    insight was Stalin's: he wanted a buffer, and he calculated — mostly correctly — that the
    West would not fight to reverse facts the Red Army had already created on the ground.
  </p>

  <h2>The American reaction: containment and its instruments</h2>
  <p>
    The United States reacted to Soviet expansionism with a strategy named for its goal:
    <strong>containment</strong> — not to roll communism back everywhere, but to stop it
    spreading further, until internal pressures transformed the USSR. Containment got three
    instruments in 1947–49. First, the <strong>Truman Doctrine</strong> (March 1947): when
    Britain could no longer fund the Greek government's fight against communist rebels or
    Turkey's defence, Truman asked Congress for $400 million, declaring US support for "free
    peoples who are resisting attempted subjugation" — the moment America made the world's
    divisions its own business. Second, the <strong>Marshall Plan</strong> (June 1947): about
    $13 billion in aid to rebuild European economies, offered to all of Europe — including the
    East — precisely because participation would force a choice; Stalin forbade the satellites
    to join, and the economic division of Europe became formal. Third, <strong>NATO</strong>
    (April 1949): a military alliance in which an attack on one was an attack on all, converting
    the American promise into a standing commitment with twelve founding members. The Soviet
    answer was the Berlin Blockade (below) and, months later, its own atomic bomb (August 1949)
    and the Warsaw Pact (1955). The architecture of the standoff was complete.
  </p>

  <h2>Berlin 1948–49: the blockade and its consequences</h2>
  <p>
    Berlin was the fault line where every Cold War fault showed. The city sat 160 km inside the
    Soviet occupation zone, but was itself divided into four sectors, like Germany. In June
    1948, when the Western powers introduced a new currency in their zones (the first step to a
    West German state), Stalin blockaded all land and water access to West Berlin —
    <strong>2.2 million people held hostage to force the West out</strong>. The Western answer
    was the <strong>Berlin Airlift</strong>: for eleven months, British and American planes flew
    in food, coal and medicine — nearly 280,000 flights, up to one landing every 45 seconds at
    the peak, supplying a city whose minimum daily need was on the order of 4,000 tonnes. Stalin,
    unwilling to shoot and start a war over supply flights, lifted the blockade in May 1949.
  </p>
  <p>
    The consequences shaped everything after. Germany was formally split: the Federal Republic
    (West) and Democratic Republic (East) were founded within months, and Berlin's divided
    status became permanent — until the wall built through it in August 1961 stopped East
    Germany's demographic hemorrhage (around 2.7 million had left through Berlin between 1949
    and 1961). Militarily the lesson learned was that <em>limited pressure works</em>: the West
    could win a confrontation without firing. Institutionally, NATO hardened and the air corridor
    precedent — and the propaganda victory was total: the blockade had made the Soviets the
    besiegers of civilians and the Americans their pizza-and-coal deliverers. "Milk for Berlin"
    did more for Western legitimacy than a division of tanks.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the Cold War was not constantly on the brink of the same
  war. Its temperature oscillated — <strong>détente</strong> in the 1970s (arms-limitation
  treaties like SALT I, the Helsinki accords) was a genuine relaxation — and most of its actual
  violence was remote and one-sided, fought by proxies. Treating all 44 years as one uniform
  "near-apocalypse" misses both the diplomacy and the millions who died in the regional wars.</p>

  <h2>Crises, proxies and the competition that built things</h2>
  <p>
    The system was tested to destruction once, in <strong>October 1962</strong>. After the US
    placed nuclear missiles in Turkey and Italy, and after the failed CIA-backed invasion of
    Cuba at the Bay of Pigs (1961), Khrushchev secretly stationed Soviet nuclear missiles in
    Cuba. A US reconnaissance plane photographed them; Kennedy chose "quarantine" (a blockade)
    over an airstrike; for thirteen days the world waited through the most dangerous fortnight in
    history — including a Soviet submarine officer, Vasili Arkhipov, refusing to consent to a
    nuclear launch. It ended with a public deal (Soviet missiles out of Cuba) and a secret one
    (US missiles out of Turkey), plus a hotline between the two capitals. Both leaders, badly
    frightened, then built the arms-control machinery — the 1963 test-ban treaty was its first
    product.
  </p>
  <p>
    Elsewhere the war stayed "hot" by proxy. Korea (1950–53) ended where it began, the 38th
    parallel, after about 3 million deaths; Vietnam (US combat troops 1965–73) killed millions
    and broke American domestic consensus; the Soviet invasion of Afghanistan (1979) became
    their Vietnam; in Angola, Ethiopia, Congo and Nicaragua, superpower patronage sustained
    clients through civil wars long past any local logic. Yet the competition also built: the
    launch of <strong>Sputnik</strong> in October 1957 shocked the US into creating NASA, whose
    Apollo programme put humans on the Moon in 1969; DARPA's ARPANET (1969), a survivable
    communications network, became the ancestor of the internet; and a good fraction of
    twentieth-century science funding was, in origin, Cold War expenditure.
  </p>

  <h2>Who was to blame? The argument the syllabus really asks</h2>
  <p>
    The question "US or USSR?" has three classic answers, and knowing them is knowing how
    historians argue. The <strong>orthodox</strong> school (Herbert Feis, and later John Lewis
    Gaddis's early work) blames Soviet expansionism: Stalin was a totalitarian who took what his
    army held. The <strong>revisionist</strong> school (William Appleman Williams, Gabriel
    Kolko, writing in the 1960s against the Vietnam backdrop) blames American economic
    expansionism: an open-door world economy that the US was determined to organise. The
    <strong>post-revisionist</strong> synthesis — today's mainstream — blames mutual
    misperception interacting with structure: both sides, seeing the other through the lens of
    their own experience (invasion for Moscow, depression for Washington), read defensive moves
    as offensive, and each step of security built for one looked like encirclement to the other.
    The opening of Soviet archives after 1991 confirmed much of the orthodox case about
    <em>Stalin</em> — the Czech coup, the Korean green light, the conviction that conflict with
    capitalism was inevitable — while also showing how much of Soviet behaviour was reactive
    fear. Gaddis's later verdict: Stalin's personality and ideology mattered enormously, and so
    did the anarchic structure neither side chose. The "blame" question, pursued honestly,
    ends in levels: individuals, states, and the system.
  </p>

  <h2>The end: 1989–1991</h2>
  <p>
    The empire built in 1945–48 came apart in barely two years, and the speed is the lesson.
    By the 1980s the Soviet economy was stagnant under the weight of central planning and
    military spending; the Reagan-era arms build-up and the 1983 Strategic Defense Initiative
    raised a price the USSR could not match. When <strong>Mikhail Gorbachev</strong> took power
    in 1985 he meant to save communism with reform: <em>glasnost</em> (openness) and
    <em>perestroika</em> (restructuring). Instead, openness exposed the system's failures and
    legitimated its critics. He then made the decisive choice: when Eastern European regimes
    wobbled in 1989, he refused to send tanks — the "Brezhnev Doctrine" of crushing dissent was
    dead. Poland's Solidarity movement won elections in June 1989; Hungary opened its border to
    Austria; and on <strong>9 November 1989 the Berlin Wall was opened and then dismantled</strong>
    by crowds. Germany reunified in 1990; the Warsaw Pact dissolved in 1991; and in December
    1991 the Soviet Union itself dissolved into fifteen states, Gorbachev resigning over
    Christmas. The map redrew itself, remarkably, without a shot exchanged between the
    superpowers — though the transition's aftermath (shock-therapy economies, the wars of
    Yugoslavia, NATO expansion debates) became this century's inheritance.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The nuclear logic deserves exact statement, because it is one of the strangest things humans
    have ever built. <strong>Mutual Assured Destruction</strong> is an equilibrium: if either
    side can destroy the other even after absorbing a first strike (guaranteed by hardened
    silos, submarines and bombers — the "nuclear triad"), then launching first is irrational,
    so neither launches, so arsenals <em>grow</em> to make the threat credible. Deterrence
    therefore required perfect performance, forever, from fallible institutions: the near-misses
    are the proof. On 26 September 1983, Soviet early-warning officer <strong>Stanislav
    Petrov</strong> judged a report of five incoming US missiles to be a satellite glitch — the
    system, it turned out, had mistaken sunlight on high-altitude clouds — and did not pass the
    alarm up. In November 1983, NATO exercise <em>Able Archer 83</em> came close to being read
    by a paranoid Soviet leadership as cover for real attack. A deterrence system that survives
    only while nothing ever goes wrong is, in the phrase historians actually use, luck-based by
    design.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Cold War also deserves reading as a <em>global</em> rather than a European story — Odd
    Arne Westad's argument. Decolonisation created dozens of new states, and the superpowers
    turned the "Third World" into the decisive chessboard, backing coups (Iran 1953, Guatemala
    1954, Chile 1973) and dictators whose local consequences outlived the contest by decades:
    the phrase "third world war" has been used for the string of proxy conflicts that killed,
    by some counts, more people than the European Cold War's famous crises ever threatened.
    Finally, its long shadow is the present: Korea's armistice line, the Taiwan question, NATO's
    membership debates, Russia's grievance narrative, and the arsenals of nine nuclear states
    are all Cold War deposits. A war that ended in 1991 without a peace treaty left its
    accounts open — which is why the discipline of asking "who was to blame?" remains a live
    civic skill, not a history-exam artefact.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Gaddis, The Cold War: A New History (2005); Westad, The Global Cold War (2005); Zubok, A Failed Empire (2007); Kennan, the "Long Telegram" (1946); Cambridge IGCSE/O Level syllabus, unit B4.</p>
